## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, the released industrial wastewater includes pollutants such as heavy metals, dyes, spilled oils, etc, and has seriously damaged water quality [1–6]. Among the reported methods to remove organic pollutants such as adsorption [7–9], membrane separation [10,11], etc., photocatalysis can permanently degrade persistent organic contaminants into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , and other nontoxic substances [12–14]. Among the reported semiconductor, such as $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ [15–17], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}[18,19]$ , CuO [20], $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ [21], $\mathsf{W O}_{3}$ [19], and $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ [22] to photodegrade organic pollutants [12,23,24], ZnO and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as typical photocatalysts have extensively gained recognition in the treatment of organic pollutants because of their low cost, nontoxicity, thermal stability, photostability, high optical capabilities, and reusability [18,22,25–27]. However, the application of pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and pure ZnO for photocatalytic degradation has some drawbacks, such as incomplete mineralization of organics of pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [12,19] and wide band gap of pure ZnO $({\sim}3.37~\mathrm{eV})$ [28–30], which greatly limit their photocatalytic performance. Furthermore, the rapid transfer of electrons $\left(\mathbf{e}^{-}\right)$ and hole $(\mathsf{h}^{+})$ plays a key role in dye degradation [31–33]. However, the quick recombination of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e}}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ greatly hinders their photocatalytic activity [34–37].
The composite of two or more semiconductors with significantly improved photocatalytic properties is a promising approach to improve the photocatalytic activity [16,38]. For example, coupling $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ with another metal oxide to prepare heterostructural nanocomposites, such as $\mathsf{A g}_{2}\mathsf{O}/\mathsf{Z n O}/\mathsf{C u O}$ [39], $Z_{\mathrm{nO/TiO_{2}}}$ [34], $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}/}$ $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ [21], and $\mathsf{Z n W O}_{4}@\mathsf{Z n O}$ [40], accelerated the separation of photoinduced electrons and holes compared with single ZnO. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has a similar large band gap to $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta[ZnO\sim3.37~eV}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\sim3.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ ), allowing the formation of nanocomposites [41,42]. $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can form heterojunction for efficient charge carrier separation. For example, Nguyen et al. [38] synthesized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{nOḌḌ/\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}$ composites through the hydrothermal method at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ and applied them for the removal of Rhodamine B (RhB) and other organic dyes. Kayaci et al. prepared $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}Z\mathrm{nO}$ composites by adopting electrospinning and explored the isolated effect of electrons $\left(\mathbf{e}^{-}\right)$ and holes $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+})$ on photocatalysis [43]. Introducing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ into ZnO increased the photocatalytic property, regulated redox energy level, and extended the carrier lifetime[44]. While those existing methods usually require more energy and reaction time, more convenient and time-saving methods should be developed.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are coordination polymers that possess high specific surface area, regular structures, and chemical diversity [45–47]. MOFs can be applied in gas storage [48], sensors [49], separation [50], and catalysis [41]. In parallel, recent works have focused on turning MOFs (as a precursor) into metal oxide nanoparticles via pyrolysis [39]. Zn-based MOF and Ti-based MOFs, including MOF-5 [51], zeolite imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) [52,53], and Ti-benzenedicarboxylate (MIL125(Ti)), were prepared as precursors of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with an improved photodegradation performances. For example, Feng et al. prepared $N\cdot$ -doped $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ with excellent photocatalytic activities by using ZIF-8 as a precursor [54]. Yang et al. have synthesized $Z_{\mathrm{{nO@C}}}$ composites via a simple method using MOF-5 as a precursor and found that the composites showed relatively higher photocatalytic decomposition activities [25]. Wang et al.
successfully fabricated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ containing carbon through the pyrolysis of ZIF-8(x) $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}.$ -MIL-125, which was synthesized by embedding an MOF in another MOF, and showed that it exerted an outstanding catalytic performance [55]. In our previous work, C-doped $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ with an enhanced photocatalytic performance were synthesized through the one-step controllable pyrolysis of MOF5 and the MOF-5 were prepared via a microwave hydrothermal method [56]. It is a simple method for oxide formation using MOF as a precursor. Besides, the composite of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Cdoped $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ can further improve the efficiency of electron-hole separation and their photocatalytic activity, which has not been reported.
In this study, the bimetal Zn/Ti-MOF were synthesized following the same method and directly calcinated to obtain C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites, in which the Zn, Ti and C element could be directly supplied by bimetal Zn/Ti-MOF. The obtained composites exhibited uniform morphology and higher efficiency photocatalysis than before. The effects of molar ratio of C-doped $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ on the photocatalytic performance were also investigated by the degradation of RhB under simulated sunlight irradiation. Compared with $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles, the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites exhibited a higher photocatalytic degradation activity to organic dyes. The photodegradation mechanism of the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites was also discussed in detail.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Catalyst preparations
$Z{\mathrm{n/Ti-MOF}}$ were prepared by the microwave hydrothermal method. In a typical process, $4.545\mathrm{~g~}(0.015\mathrm{~mol})$ of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.$  $6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and $_{1.246\mathrm{~g~}}$ $(0.0075~\mathrm{\mol})$ terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ as organic linker were first dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ N,N-dimethylforma mide (DMF) and stirred at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $5~\mathrm{\min}$ . Subsequently, $0.104{\mathrm{~mL}}$ $(0.0003\mathrm{~mol})_{.}^{\cdot}$ butyl titanate and $3{\mathrm{~mL}}$ methanol were mixed into the above solution. After stirring at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ the mixed solution was transferred into a microwave hydrothermal synthesis system at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . As the reactor was cooled down to $\sim25~^{\circ}C$ the mixture was washed five times with ethanol and then dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 10 h. Zn/Ti-MOF with a molar ratio of Zn to $\mathrm{Ti}$ of $2\%$ was prepared and labeled as $2/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%$ -MOF. The information of other molar ratios of $Z\mathrm{n}$ to Ti of Zn/Ti-MOF are listed in Table 1.
The C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ composites with a molar ratio of Zn to Ti of $2\%$ were obtained by the direct calcination of above Zn/Ti-MOF. After drying, $500~\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared $Z\mathrm{n/Ti}$ -MOF powders was transferred into a muffle furnace and reacted at $600~^{\circ}C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ under air (the heating rate: $5~{^{\circ}C}/\mathrm{min})$ . The C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites with a molar ratio of Zn to Ti of $2\%$ were obtained and labeled as $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ . For comparison, pure $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $C-$ doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites with other molar ratios of Zn to Ti (Z/T- $1\%$ -600, Z/T- $2\%$ -600, Z/T-5%-600 and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}10\%{-}600\rangle$ and other calcination temperatures (400, 500, 600 and $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were prepared in the same manner. The relevant molar ratios of Zn to Ti of the Cdoped $Z_{\mathrm{nO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}$ composites and their abbreviations are shown in Table 1 (X is calcination temperature and $\Chi=400{,}500$ , 600 and 700) (see Table 2.).
## 2.2. Photocatalytic degradation
The photocatalytic performance of the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ composites was assessed via the removal of RhB under continuous simulated sunlight irradiation, and the experiments of dark adsorption were conducted. Exactly $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-prepared C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}/$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composites were dispersed to the photochemical reactor containing RhB solution $10\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ ) and kept in the dark for $30\mathrm{min}$ prior to the irradiations to reach the adsorption–desorption equilibrium. Then, the mixture was illuminated using a 200 W xenon lamp (CEL-LPH120) with the wavelength range of $320-$ $1100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ as a solar-simulated light source. Meanwhile, approximately $5\mathrm{mL}$ solution was taken out at regular intervals for the concentration detection of RhB. The RhB absorbance was obtained with a Shanghai UV-9000 absorption spectrophotometer at the maximum absorption wavelength of $554~\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. X-ray diffraction
Fig. 1A shows the XRD patterns of the samples. The characteristic peak at $2\uptheta=6.96^{\circ}$ , $9.8^{\circ}$ , $13.84^{\circ}$ , and $15.46^{\circ}$ observed from MOF$5,Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -MOF, $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}5\%$ -MOF, and $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}10\%$ -MOF correspond to the MOF-5 crystal planes. In addition, no peaks of Ti-MOF appear, which may be due to the low content of Ti. The characteristic diffraction peaks of $Z_{\mathrm{nO-}600}$ , $2/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%$ -600, $2/\mathrm{T}{-}5\%$ -600, and $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}$ $10\%$ -600 at $31.77^{\circ}$ , $34.43^{\circ}$ , $36.26^{\circ}$ , $47.55^{\circ}$ , $56.61^{\circ}$ , $62.87^{\circ}$ , and $67.96^{\circ}$ can be assigned to the (1 0 0), (0 0 2), (1 0 1), (1 0 2), (1 1 0), (1 0 3), and (1 1 2) crystal planes of the tetragonal phase ZnO, which can be referred to the international center for diffraction data (ICDD) number #98-002-9272 [57]. No obvious peaks are observed from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ possibly because of the low content of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in the composites [22,58]. The effect of calcination temperature on crystalline nature was also measured (Fig. 1B). Only $Z/\mathrm{T}$ - $2\text{\textperthousand}$ retains the diffraction peaks of MOF-5 and ZnO. Meanwhile, the peaks belonging to ZnO become higher and stronger as the temperature is increased up to over $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . This result demonstrates that the crystallization of the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites increases with increasing the calcination temperature.
## 3.2. SEM analysis
The morphologies of the as-prepared composites are illustrated in Fig. 2A-F. $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -MOF (Fig. 2A) and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ (Fig. 2C) exhibit a cubic morphology but show an average edge length of ${\sim}15$ and ${\sim}1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively. As evident in Fig. 2B, $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -MOF shows a smooth surface at higher magnifications, but the surface of $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}$ $2\%-600$ (Fig. 2D) displays a particle-like structure. The morphologies of the samples are significantly influenced by the calcination temperature due to the loss of guest molecules and the decomposition of organic components during calcination. Compared with $Z/$ $\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ , ZnO-600 (Fig. 2E and 2F) shows an irregularly cube structure and a much larger edge length of ${\sim}25~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Furthermore, SEM–elemental mapping image (Fig. 2H–J) and EDS spectra (Fig. 3) of $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -600 reveal that Ti, Zn, O, and C are uniformly distributed over $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -600. The $\mathrm{Ti}/\mathrm{Zn}$ atom ratio and weight ratio in $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%.$ - 600 catalysts are $1.5\%$ and $1.1\%$ respectively, which are slightly lower than the actual amount of raw materials added and the details of other element are shown in Fig. 3.
## 3.3. TEM analysis
As shown in Fig. 4, the TEM images of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ are used to observe the morphology and crystal structures of C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}/$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Fig. 4A shows that $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ contains aggregated nanoparticles with a length of $50{-}100~\mathrm{nm}$ . The HRTEM micrographs of $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}$ $2\%-600$ with clear lattice stripes are shown in Fig. 4B–4D. Four lattice fringe spacings of 0.26, 0.22, 0.202, and $0.27{\mathrm{nm}}$ correspond to the (1 0 2) plane of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , the (1 1 1) plane of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the (1 0 1) plane of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the (1 0 2) plane of C, suggesting that C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}/}$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composites were successfully synthesized.
## 3.4. XPS analysis
XPS measurement was performed to study the elemental states of Zn, Ti, O, and C in C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites. The full-scan XPS spectrum of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ is displayed in Fig. 5A, and the main elements of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ are Zn, Ti, O, and C. From Fig. 5B, the two peaks of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ can be assigned to $Z\mathrm{n}~2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ $(1020.8~\mathrm{eV})$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ (1043.8 eV), respectively [51]. Furthermore, a new peak located at 1021.1 eV $(\mathsf{Z n~2p3}/2)$ of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ could be attributed to $z_{\mathrm{{n-C}}}$ bonds (C-doping) [34,59]. Fig. 5C shows three peaks of the Ti $\mathsf{2p}$ XPS spectrum, which can be assigned to Ti $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ $(458.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ , $Z_{\mathrm{{nTiO}_{3}}}$ $(460~\mathrm{eV})$ , and Ti $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ (464.1 eV)[60]. The C1s XPS spectra are depicted in Fig. 5D, and C1s XPS can be fitted into four characteristic peaks. The major peak centered at $284.6\mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to the adventitious hydrocarbon [15], whereas the minor peak at 284.1 eV is attributed to the $Z\mathrm{n-C}$ bonds (C-doping). In addition, the C-doped amount for $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ is about $4.15\%$ [34,56]. The two other peaks centered at binding energies of 286.1 and 288.7 eV are assigned to bound carbonate species $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=}0,-\mathbf{C}=0$ and $-\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ )[59]. In addition, we investigated the O1s XPS spectrum (Fig. 5E) of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ Results show that the peak positioned at $529.8{\mathrm{~eV}}$ is due to the lattice oxygen anions $(0^{2-})$ . The peak at $531.3\mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to loosely bound oxygen species, such as CAO, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , and OH groups [50,51]. The results illustrate that $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ consists of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ .
## 3.5. BET analysis
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption desorption isotherms of the samples are shown in Fig. 6. The type-III isotherms with a type H1 hysteresis loop demonstrate the mesoporous nature of all samples. The specific surface area of $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -MOF $(732.49\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) is considerably larger than $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -600 $\cdot10.66\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}5\%$ -600 ( $15.49\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ), and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}5\%$ -600 $(20.20\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ . This is due to the loss of guest molecules and the decomposition of organic components during calcination, which is in accordance with the results of electron microscopic analysis. The pore volumes of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ $\cdot0.026\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ - $\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ), $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}5\%{-}600$ $\mathrm{?0.051~cm^{3}g^{-1}}.$ ), and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}10\%{-}600$ $(0.072~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.\$ ) are much smaller than that of $2/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%$ -MOF. Meanwhile, their pore size distributions are all within $2-14~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which provides efficient transport pathways in the photocatalysis.
## 3.6. UV–vis analysis
UV–vis absorption spectra of composites are presented in Fig. 7A. $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -MOF and MOF-5 show the absorption edge of $\sim325\ \mathrm{nm}$ , whereas $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{2/T}\mathrm{-}2\%\mathrm{-}600,\mathrm{~Z}/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}5\%\mathrm{-}600,\mathrm{~Z}/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}10\%\mathrm{-}600\mathrm{~ZnO},}\end{array}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ show a wide absorption edge at approximately $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ wavelength, which expands the light absorption range. Among the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ composites, $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ exhibits the strongest absorbance. The $E_{g}$ values of the samples were estimated based on the Formula (1) and summarized in Table S1 [61]:
$$
\alpha h\nu=K{\left(h\nu-E_{g}\right)}^{1/2}
$$
where $\alpha,h\nu,K,$ and $E g$ represent the absorption coefficient, photon energy, proportionality constant, and band gap energy, respectively. The bandgap energy values of MOF-5 and $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -MOF are 3.98 and $3.92\ \mathrm{eV},$ , respectively, which are both larger than those of other products. Meanwhile, the band gap of $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -600, $2/\mathrm{T}{-}5\%{-}600,Z/$ T- $10\%$ -600, ZnO-600 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}–600$ decreases to 3.25, 3.2, 3.18, 3.12, and $2.95\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Reducing the band gap energy of the photocatalysts can promise more full utilization of solar energy, which is conducive to the transition of electrons under visible-light irradiation and enhancement of photocatalytic performance [21].
## 3.7. PL analysis
The PL spectra of ZnO-600, Z/T- $2\%$ -600, $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}5\%{-}600$ and Z/T$10\%$ -600 are displayed in Fig. 8 to evaluate the electron/hole recombination rate. Notably, compared with other as-synthesized products, $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ exhibits a lower PL intensity, indicating higher efficiency of electron-hole separation, which can improve photocatalytic efficiency. This result is consistent with the photocatalytic degradation performance [62].
## 3.8. Photocurrent measurement
The photocurrent measurement against the time of different photoelectrodes was recorded via several on-off cycles of the periodic 200 W Xenon lamp light irradiation pulse of $20~s.$ Fig. 9 provides the transient photocurrent responses of the $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600,Z/$ $T\mathrm{-}5\%$ -600, $2/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%$ -MOF, $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}600}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}–600$ . The $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ photoelectrode demonstrated a stronger transient photocurrent response under the simulated sunlight irradiation, which was consistent with its better photocatalytic performance.
## 3.9. Photocatalytic activity
The degradation of RhB $\cdot10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) was studied to evaluate the effect of the Ti-to-Zn ratios in the composite on photocatalytic properties, and $30\ \mathrm{min}$ dark adsorption was conducted to reach adsorption–desorption equilibrium. As shown in Fig. 10A, the blank test of RhB does not show a noticeable removal rate, illustrating that photodegradation does not occur without a photocatalyst. After $45\ \mathrm{\min}$ of the simulated sunlight irradiation, the photocatalytic degradation rates of RhB $\mathrm{10~mg~L^{-1}},$ ) over $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%.$ -
MOF, $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}1\%$ -600, $2/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%$ -600, Z/T- $.5\%$ -600, Z/T- $10\%$ -600, $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}600}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}–600$ are $23\%$ , $73\%$ , $96\%$ , $90\%$ , $74\%$ , $69\%$ , and $34\%$ , respectively. All the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites outperformed their precursors as well as $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-}}600}$ , and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -600, suggesting that Cdoped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites have notably enhanced the photocatalytic ability. In addition, $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ exhibits the highest photocatalytic capacity among the products.
Furthermore, the apparent reaction rate constant $(k)$ can be calculated by Eq. (2) [63]:
$$
l n(C_{0}/C_{t})=k t
$$
where $C_{0}(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and $C_{t}\left(\mathrm{mg.L^{-1}}\right)$ are the concentrations of RhB at the beginning and time t (min), respectively, and $k$ is the rate constant. As shown in Fig. 10B, the degradation of RhB follows a pseudo-first-order kinetic behavior, and the $k$ values of $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%.$ - MOF, ZnO-600, Z/T-1%-600, Z/T- $2\%$ -600, Z/T- $5\%$ -600, Z/T-10%-600, and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -600 are 0.003, 0.024, 0.029, 0.063, 0.045, 0.029, and $0.009\quad\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , respectively. The highest $k$ is $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -600 $(0.063\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ). Fig. 10C and 10D compares the effect of calcination temperature on the photocatalytic performance. $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ shows a better degradation efficiency and higher $k$ than $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -400, $Z/\mathrm{T}$ - $2\text{\textperthousand}$ , and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}700$ after $45\ \mathrm{min}$ of simulated sunlight irradiation. This is possibly because that the excessively high temperature will reduce the contents of C doping. Furthermore, the values of $k$ and correlation coefficient $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$ are listed in Table S2. Table 3 shows that the $Z_{\mathrm{n}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ composites prepared in this study possess a better photocatalytic performance and less reaction time for precursors compared with other reported $Z_{\mathrm{n}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ composites. The C doping can act as an electron trapper in photocatalysis and provide a quick charge transfer and higher separation efficiency in the photocatalytic degradation process, which can improve the photocatalytic efficiency of $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites.
## 3.10. Stability test
The reusability of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ was further tested for RhB removal during 5 recycling runs, and the recovery of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ was obtained by centrifugal separation, washing with ethanol, and dried at $70~^{\circ}C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ . Fig. 11 shows that the photocatalytic activity of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ after five cycles exhibits no significant change in the RhB degradation. The XRD pattern and SEM analysis of the samples after five cycles are studied to explore its stability. The XRD pattern of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ does not show any obvious change after the photocatalytic reaction and exhibits a cubic morphology and the morphology of recovered $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ does not show obvious changes compared with the pristine $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%$ -600, indicating the high stability and reusability of $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ .
## 3.11. Photocatalytic mechanism
The conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZnO were calculated according to Equations (3&4) [65,66]:
$$
\begin{array}{r c l}{E_{C B}=X-E^{C}-0.5E_{g}}\\ {\ }&{\ }\\ {E_{V B}=X-\mathrm{E}^{\mathrm{c}}+0.5\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}}\end{array}
$$
where $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ and $E_{\mathsf{V B}}$ are the CB and VB edge potentials, respectively; $X$ is the electronegativity (5.94 and 5.89 for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ respectively) [22]; $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the band gap energy (3.12 and $2.95\ \mathrm{eV}$ for ZnO and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively); and $E^{\mathrm{C}}$ is the energy of free electron on the hydrogen scale (4.5 eV) [22]. The CB and VB band edge potentials of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ can be calculated to be $-0.12$ and $3.00\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, and those of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are $-0.085$ and $2.86\:\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively.
Isopropyl (IPA) and $p$ -benzoquinone (BQ) were adopted as radical scavengers by measuring OH and $\cdot_{\mathrm{0}_{2}^{-}}$ , respectively, to investigate the reactive agents and probe the photocatalytic mechanism.
In addition, EDTA-2Na served as a trapping agent to detect hole quenchers $(\mathsf{h}^{+})$ . As presented in Fig. 12, the addition of scavengers caused an obvious inhibition. Typically, the degradation rates of RhB aqueous solution decreased from $96\%$ to $21.9\%$ , $35.8\%$ and $18.8\%$ in the presence of EDTA-2Na, IPA, and $\mathsf{B Q}$ respectively, indicating that $\cdot_{\mathbf{0}_{2}^{-}}$ is the main reactive agent. Meanwhile, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ and OH played crucial roles in RhB degradation.
The production of radicals in the reaction system was detected through the electron spin resonance (ESR) technique using 5,5- dimethyl-pyrroline $N\mathrm{.}$ -oxide (DMPO) as a capturing agent to investigate the existence of $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ (Fig. 13). Results reveal that no obvious signals for DMPO- $\cdot_{\mathbf{0}_{2}^{-}}$ were observed in the dark, whereas apparent signals of the $\cdot_{\mathbf{0}_{2}^{-}}$ radical increase with prolonged visible light irradiation time $(0{-}10\ \operatorname{min})$ . Consequently, $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ was produced during the RhB photodegradation.
The reaction process can be described approximately by the following Eqs. (5)–(11):
$$
\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\stackrel{\mathrm{visible~light}}{\longrightarrow}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\left(\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{h}^{+}\right)
$$
$$
\mathsf{Z n O}\overset{\mathrm{visible\:\light}}{\longrightarrow}\mathsf{Z n O}(\mathsf{e}^{-}+\mathsf{h}^{+})
$$
$$
\mathrm{TiO}_{2}(\mathrm{e}^{-})+\mathrm{O}_{2}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}+\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}{^{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{ZnO}\left(\mathrm{e}^{-}\right)+\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{\rightarrow}\mathrm{ZnO}+\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}
$$
$$
\mathrm{TiO}_{2}(\mathrm{h^{+}})+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\ +\ \cdot\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{H^{+}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{ZnO(h^{+})+H_{2}O{\rightarrow}Z n O+\cdot O H+H^{+}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{RhB+\left\{\begin{array}{l}{{\cdot O_{2}^{-}}}\\ {{h^{+}}}\\ {{\cdot O H}}\end{array}\right\}\to C O_{2}+H_{2}O+o t h e r\mathrm{products}}
$$
With the consideration of aforementioned experimental results and related literature, the photocatalytic mechanism of C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}/}$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composites is schematically shown in Fig. 14. It can be analyzed by three steps. First, under simulated sunlight irradiation, $\mathsf{e}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ are generated from the VB to the CB of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively. However, fast charge recombination between electron–hole pairs can significantly influence the efficiency of photocatalysis, and the doped C can act as an electron trapper in photocatalysis. The $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ value of ZnO $(-0.12\ \mathrm{eV})$ is more negative than that of $\Gamma\mathrm{i}0_{2}\left(-0.085\mathrm{eV}\right)$ . Therefore, a small number of electrons move from the CB of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to the CB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and the holes migrate from the VB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to the VB of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , which will also contribute to charge separation [34,50]. The holes $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+})$ are left in the VB, while the $0_{2}$ and OH in RhB aqueous solution can be converted into $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ and OH by e and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , respectively. Given their strong oxidizing properties, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ and OH can decompose RhB into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ , and other non-toxic products [27].
## 4. Conclusion
MOF-derived C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{nO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}$ composites with highly photocatalytic properties were successfully synthesized by a facile microwave hydrothermal and controllable calcination process. The as-prepared $Z/\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-}2\%.$ -MOF and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ exhibit regular cubic morphology with diameters of $\sim15$ and $\sim1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , respectively. In addition, under the $45\ \mathrm{min}$ simulated sunlight irradiation, $94\%$ of RhB is degraded when $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ is used as the photocatalyst, which is superior to the other $Z/T{-}n{-}600$ . The RhB degradation promoted by the C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites follows the pseudofirst-order kinetic process, and $Z/\mathrm{T}{-}2\%{-}600$ shows high stability and reusability after recycling for five times. Furthermore, $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , and OH play crucial roles in the RhB degradation. This study provides a facile approach to prepare C-doped $Z_{\mathrm{n}}0/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ composites with less reaction time. The proposed approach also shows high potential application value to remove various types of dyes from the effluents.
